

like an empty tube of toothpaste

this is the dialect of God searching 
the empty plastic bag of my body 
for heartbeats. 

the story only begins 
when you remember it.
 
at twenty-five a friend convinced me 
to bind my life's story in a manuscript
and take it like a foreign movie 
to my therapist for subtitles. 

on the phone call 
I shredded my history into bits
like the green vegetables
I chopped beside my mother
making soup at sixteen.

i was desperate to give mother 
a life where her prayers weren't fumes rising 
every morning from an old kettle

and my siblings wouldn't sift cereal
from bowls of milk, as I sifted words
from my dictionary,

stuffing the open mouth 
of hunger
with Scrabble tiles.

now the gate at home swings in the wind
and behind it is a boy praying for escape, 
waiting to welcome me home.   






